Here's how to handle draft persistence with .NET MAUI's lifecycle events:

**In your `App.xaml.cs`** — hook into the platform lifecycle:

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    public App()
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        MainPage = new AppShell();
    }

    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
    {
        var window = base.CreateWindow(activationState);

        window.Deactivated += (s, e) =>
        {
            // Fires when app goes to background
            MessagingCenter.Send<App>(this, "SaveDraft");
        };

        window.Activated += (s, e) =>
        {
            // Fires when app returns to foreground
            MessagingCenter.Send<App>(this, "RestoreDraft");
        };

        return window;
    }
}
```

**In your note-taking page** (`NotePage.xaml.cs`):

```csharp
public partial class NotePage : ContentPage
{
    private const string DraftKey = "note_draft";

    public NotePage()
    {
        InitializeComponent();

        MessagingCenter.Subscribe<App>(this, "SaveDraft", (sender) =>
        {
            Preferences.Set(DraftKey, NoteEditor.Text ?? string.Empty);
        });

        MessagingCenter.Subscribe<App>(this, "RestoreDraft", (sender) =>
        {
            // Only restore if editor is empty (user hasn't started fresh)
            if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(NoteEditor.Text))
                NoteEditor.Text = Preferences.Get(DraftKey, string.Empty);
        });

        // Also restore on first load
        NoteEditor.Text = Preferences.Get(DraftKey, string.Empty);
    }

    protected override void OnDisappearing()
    {
        base.OnDisappearing();
        MessagingCenter.Unsubscribe<App>(this, "SaveDraft");
        MessagingCenter.Unsubscribe<App>(this, "RestoreDraft");
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

| Concern | Approach |
|---------|----------|
| Storage | `Preferences` — lightweight, synchronous, ideal for small text |
| Lifecycle | `Window.Deactivated` / `Activated` events |
| Communication | `MessagingCenter` decouples App from Page |
| Cleanup | Unsubscribe in `OnDisappearing` to avoid leaks |

**Modern alternative** (if targeting .NET 8+, `MessagingCenter` is deprecated):

Replace `MessagingCenter` with `WeakReferenceMessenger` from the CommunityToolkit.Mvvm NuGet package:

```csharp
// Send
WeakReferenceMessenger.Default.Send(new DraftMessage("save"));

// Subscribe
WeakReferenceMessenger.Default.Register<DraftMessage>(this, (r, m) => { ... });
```

For drafts larger than ~4KB, swap `Preferences` for `SecureStorage` or write to `FileSystem.AppDataDirectory`.
